WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.880 --> 00:00:09.040
Just before his army invaded Ukraine, Vladimir&nbsp;
Putin outlined his motivations in a speech.

00:00:10.400 --> 00:00:15.920
His first and main argument:

00:00:18.720 --> 00:00:22.640
He blamed the extension of the military&nbsp;
alliance ever closer to Russia’s borders&nbsp;

00:00:23.520 --> 00:00:29.840
and accused Western leaders of breaking&nbsp;
alleged promises to never do so

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.600
But experts disagree on whether that’s true

00:00:37.600 --> 00:00:41.360
the U.S. broke these assurances that had been made

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:43.840
I think it’s false. It’s really sort of a pretext

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:49.520
So was anything really promised?
And why is that such a crucial point for Russia?

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:02.160
After WWII two spheres of influence emerged in&nbsp;
Europe, each one with their military alliance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:07.920
NATO in the west, countries that would soon&nbsp;
become part of the Warsaw Pact in the east.

00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:12.560
This created a clear red line&nbsp;
between the two superpowers.&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:17.200
Their spheres of influence were clearly&nbsp;
defined and for many years NATO grew slowly&nbsp;

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:24.000
– until 1990, when that divide&nbsp;
started to crumble…here in Germany

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:29.200
Germany was the key focal point&nbsp;
of the Cold War in Europe.

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:35.360
Joshua Shifrinson has done extensive archival&nbsp;
research on the U.S. and Soviet foreign policy

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:40.400
including this great question of&nbsp;
what was or was not promised in 1990

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:45.760
At the time, Germany was speeding toward&nbsp;
reunification. And for many in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:46.880
this was a bit scary.

00:01:49.840 --> 00:01:57.440
The US did not want to see a reunified&nbsp;
Germany either in the Soviet camp or neutral.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.440 --> 00:02:01.360
And the concern with neutrality was that&nbsp;
a neutral Germany might one day run amok.

00:02:01.360 --> 00:02:05.520
Washington decided the best option&nbsp;
was to bring Germany into NATO.&nbsp;

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.240
But first, they had to convince the Soviets.&nbsp;

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:12.000
And here we get to the famous&nbsp;
assurances about NATO’s expansion.

00:02:17.840 --> 00:02:23.360
in February of 1990, in a series of meetings,&nbsp;
U.S. and West German leaders very openly said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:31.760
that if the Soviet leadership consented to German&nbsp;
reunification and Germany remaining in NATO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.400 --> 00:02:35.840
then NATO would not expand one inch to the East

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:40.800
Logs of those conversations&nbsp;
have since been declassified.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:50.800
And they show what Soviet politicians were&nbsp;
told by western leaders like… (show several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.320 --> 00:03:03.840
of the documents)

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:06.640
So, what happened to NATO after Germany unified?

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:17.040
In 1999 the Czech Republic,&nbsp;
Hungary, and Poland joined NATO&nbsp;

00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:20.880
In 2004 it was the baltic&nbsp;
states and four other countries&nbsp;

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.440
And finally four more from&nbsp;
2009 to just a few years ago&nbsp;

00:03:25.440 --> 00:03:29.520
Georgia and Ukraine were promised&nbsp;
membership in 2008 but are not yet members

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:32.720
This would seem to justify Putin’s grievances.&nbsp;

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.720
But one historical event could&nbsp;
suggest a different interpretation:

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:39.680
Back in 1991 and the collapse of the Soviet Union

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:45.760
This is Steven Pifer.He was the US ambassador&nbsp;
to Ukraine when NATO was expanding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.760 --> 00:03:49.120
and deputy director on the Soviet&nbsp;
desk when those assurances were made.

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:53.440
When Secretary Baker at that time&nbsp;
was talking about not extending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:57.120
forward. I understood not into East Germany

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:03.520
Pifer says that those commitments didn’t refer&nbsp;
to countries like Poland or Lithuania because…

00:04:03.520 --> 00:04:08.640
and at that time, the Warsaw Pact still&nbsp;
existed. It didn't collapse for another year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:13.840
so I saw the discussions about not one&nbsp;
inch really in the context of Germany.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:15.760
But many experts disagree

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:21.360
That doesn't quite hold water for two&nbsp;
different reasons. One is that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.360 --> 00:04:25.120
Soviet leadership and the Americans&nbsp;
both understood that the question of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.040
what comes next in Europe was all about the&nbsp;
future of Eastern Europe, not East Germany.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:33.840
There's a concern that the Warsaw Pact is&nbsp;
falling apart. And also, if I say to you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:39.360
I'm not going east. It's a pretty generic&nbsp;
statement, right? It kind of beggars the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:43.840
imagination to think that they only meant, oh,&nbsp;
it's only there, but no further from there.

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.640
Pifer, however, raises another point

00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:52.960
When you look at the actual agreements, there&nbsp;
were no final commitments by NATO not to enlarge.

00:04:52.960 --> 00:04:55.840
In other words, no treaties. Only conversations.

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:00.080
Nothing was written down, but at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:06.640
diplomatic commitments carry a lot of water&nbsp;
in politics. This is how we ended the Cuban&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.640 --> 00:05:12.560
Missile Crisis. And if it didn't matter, why&nbsp;
would we have diplomats talk to each other?

00:05:12.560 --> 00:05:15.360
Regardless of how serious these assurances were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:18.400
this debate arguably misses&nbsp;
a more important point.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.400
States don't uphold treaties just&nbsp;
because they're written down.&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.080
If those leaders in those states decide to&nbsp;
change course, then a treaty is not going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.080 --> 00:05:37.840
stand in the way. So all the emphasis on writing&nbsp;
things down… it puts the cart before the horse.

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:42.400
NATO’s expansion could be&nbsp;
better explained by focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:45.440
not on those conversations&nbsp;
but on the countries involved.

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:51.840
On one side, the U.S., whose influence&nbsp;
grew after the fall of the Soviet Union.

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.480
The NATO expansion was fundamentally&nbsp;
a byproduct of the unipolar era.&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.200
Right of this moment in time where the U.S.&nbsp;
had great power, very few constraints, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:07.520
at least some policymakers who had an idea that&nbsp;
American power and influence applied to the world&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.520 --> 00:06:10.800
would generate lots of goods for the United&nbsp;
States, as well as good things for the world.

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:14.080
On another, the countries that joined NATO

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:20.320
It wasn't that NATO's said, let's enlarge. It was&nbsp;
you had countries such as Poland emerging from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:25.120
the records of the Warsaw Pact saying, we want&nbsp;
to be a member of NATO, we want to be a member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:30.480
of the European Union, and we are prepared&nbsp;
to do all of the things that are required.

00:06:31.840 --> 00:06:35.680
And finally, Russia, whose&nbsp;
motivations are not limited to NATO

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:37.360
I think they're really three reasons behind what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:38.880
Russia's doing.
1 - One is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.880 --> 00:06:43.440
the geopolitical concern. The Russians want a&nbsp;
sphere of influence in the post-Soviet space,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.440 --> 00:06:51.920
and Ukraine is a very important part of that.
2 - One is a domestic political concern.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.960 --> 00:07:00.640
For the Kremlin, a western-oriented&nbsp;
democratic, economically successful Ukraine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.640 --> 00:07:05.680
is a nightmare for the Kremlin because it&nbsp;
leads to Russians asking, ‘why can't we have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:09.920
the same political voice and the same&nbsp;
democratic rights that they have in Ukraine?&nbsp;

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:14.560
3 - And then there's a third risk, which&nbsp;
I just think is it's Vladimir Putin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:18.320
and he's quite emotional about Ukraine.&nbsp;
He's very emotional and even angry about it.

00:07:26.240 --> 00:07:30.080
Whether NATO should have kept&nbsp;
its assurances is up for debate

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:34.800
We underestimated just how much antipathy&nbsp;
there was in Moscow to NATO's existence.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:41.200
Years of the US are downplaying the reassurances&nbsp;
that were previously given and making Russia feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:41.200 --> 00:07:45.520
like it could not trust the West have really&nbsp;
narrowed the opportunity to resolve this crisis

00:07:47.360 --> 00:07:50.640
But it's really sort of a pretext now.
Mr. Putin has put together this list of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.640 --> 00:07:54.320
grievances against the West, But he&nbsp;
didn't talk about them 20 years ago.

00:07:56.000 --> 00:08:01.440
But those discussions were important not only for&nbsp;
what they promised but also for what they showed:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.880 --> 00:08:06.320
a willingness to compromise and to&nbsp;
not cross each other’s red lines.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:15.120
Over the years, the situation around Russia&nbsp;
changed, and more countries decided to join NATO

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.520
But it changed even more within Russia,&nbsp;
as Putin’s grip on power strengthened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:33.360
and he became more authoritarian and isolated

